Interviewer: Hello, do you have some free time? Are you a UQ student? We developed a product to help students find UQ clubs more easily. You can check the club information here. If you are interested in a club like the soccer club, click it to view details including location, time and email. After joining, it will show on your profile, and you can receive club notifications here. Thanks for testing our platform today. To start, what was your first impression when you opened it?

Interviewee: Yeah, my first impression was honestly pretty positive. I noticed the welcome banner with purple and gold color scheme, which is UQ's branding. It feels official and trustworthy. The layout is clean, with a search bar on the top, category tabs and recommended clubs below. I didn't feel overwhelmed, and I could tell what the app is for within a few seconds. It is modern and well-designed.

Interviewer: OK, sure, that's great to hear. So, how easy was it to find a club that matched your interest?

Interviewee: To be honest, it's very easy. The homepage already lists recommended clubs, such as the UQ football club, the Malaysia Student Association and Computing Society. As an international student, the Malaysia Student Association caught my eye immediately right on the front page. For general interests, recommendations work well. If I have a very specific hobby, I will use the search function.

Interviewer: OK, sure. Does the search function and club categories help you discover relevant clubs?

Interviewee: The categories are definitely helpful. There are four main types: sports, society, culture and others, with corresponding icons. The club page also keeps the same category tags, so the design is consistent. The search bar works well when I type club names. I prefer using categories when I don't have a clear target in mind.

Interviewer: OK. Were there any part of the interface that you found confusing or difficult to use?

Interviewee: Mostly it works smoothly, but there are a couple of small issues. I wasn't sure if the recommended clubs on the homepage are personalized or just repeat the full club list, so there is some ambiguity. Besides, the pop-up window for club details has no obvious close button. I had to find the small X icon on the corner. It would be better to add a clear close button or allow users to tap outside the window to close it. These are just minor problems and won't stop me from using the platform.

Interviewer: OK. Did the club detail page provide enough information for you to decide whether to join a club?

Interviewee: Yes, it does a very good job. Take the Malaysian student association as an example. The card shows category tag, member count, introduction, weekly activities, meeting location and contact email. The only thing I hope to add is upcoming event information.

Interviewer: OK. How easy was it to navigate between different pages and features?

Interviewee: Very easy. There is a top navigation bar with three clear tabs: Home, Clubs and Profiles. The active page is highlighted, so I always know where I am. I can switch freely between pages. The profile page displays personal information, the number of joined clubs, a section for joined clubs and a notification area. The overall structure is reasonable, and the navigation is excellent.

Interviewer: OK. How useful do you think this platform would be for new UQ students looking to get involved on campus?

Interviewee: I think it would be very useful, especially for freshmen and international students. They usually know nothing about campus clubs and how to join them. This platform puts all clubs in one place with clear categories, lowering the access barrier. It is a perfect starting point for new students who feel confused about campus activities.

Interviewer: Compare to other ways of finding clubs, such as UQ websites, social media and asking friends, what's your impression?

Interviewee: This platform is better in terms of organization. UQ official websites are confusing with information scattered on different pages. Content on social media is random and hard to find. Advice from friends is limited to their own knowledge. This platform integrates all club resources with unified information, which is its biggest advantage. If it can add real-time updates and posts like social media, it will be more competitive. Its club discovery function is already outstanding.

Interviewer: OK. Last question. If this platform were officially available, how likely would you be to use it in the future and why?

Interviewee: I would rate it 8 out of 10. It solves a real problem in a simple way. I can find and compare clubs without trouble. I will definitely use it at the beginning of each semester to browse and join clubs, and check it when I want new activities. Long-term use depends on whether functions like event calendars can keep the platform active after users sign up. But for its original purpose, I am satisfied and I will recommend it to other students. It is worthy of official launch.

Interviewer: OK, thank you so much for your valuable feedback. Have a good day.

Interviewee: Bye bye.
